The same happens with `WordprocessingDocument` and `PresentationDocument` (same package code). I also saw the same file-on-disk change when the package is opened read-only through a library that uses the SDK.

**Observed behavior**

- `Open(path, isEditable: false)` writes to the file: the `.rels` part now contains `rewritten://<GUID>`, the original address is lost, and the write time changes.
- On a file with the read-only attribute set, the same call throws `ObjectDisposedException: Package object was closed and disposed...` which presumably comes from the SDK reopening the file for writing (see the analysis below).

**Expected behavior**

A read-only open never writes to the original file. If the SDK needs to rewrite relationships to be able to load the package, it should do so on the in-memory copy it already creates for read-only packages (see `EnableWriteableStream`), which is what happens for `Open(Stream, false)`.

**Analysis**

For read-only packages, `EnableUriHandling` calls `EnableWriteableStream()`, which swaps the package stream for a temp copy so the rewrite never reaches the original. `MalformedUriHandlingPackage.WriteStream` then calls `Reload(access: FileAccess.ReadWrite)`. `FilePackageFeature.GetStream(mode, access)` handles that by disposing the current stream and doing `File.Open(_path, mode, access, ...)` on the **original path**, so the temp copy is bypassed and the rewritten `.rels` is written to the real file. `Dispose` of the handler returns early when `FileOpenAccess == Read`, so the original targets are never put back.

**Suggested fix**

Do not reopen the path once the stream has been replaced by the writable copy:

```diff
diff --git a/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/FilePackageFeature.cs b/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/FilePackageFeature.cs
index aa6b429d..3f75f65f 100644
--- a/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/FilePackageFeature.cs
+++ b/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/FilePackageFeature.cs
@@ -10,11 +10,13 @@ namespace DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Features;
 internal sealed class FilePackageFeature : StreamPackageFeature, IPackageStreamFeature
 {
     private readonly string _path;
+    private readonly Stream _original;

     public FilePackageFeature(string path, PackageOpenMode mode)
         : base(CreateStream(path, mode), mode, isOwned: true)
     {
         _path = path;
+        _original = Stream;
     }

     private static FileStream CreateStream(string path, PackageOpenMode openMode)
@@ -34,7 +36,7 @@ private static FileStream CreateStream(string path, PackageOpenMode openMode)

     protected override Stream GetStream(FileMode mode, FileAccess access)
     {
-        if (_path is null)
+        if (_path is null || !ReferenceEquals(Stream, _original))
         {
             return Stream;
         }
```

With this change, the repro above leaves the file untouched (bytes and write time), a file with the read-only attribute opens fine, and an editable open + edit + dispose still restores the original target. I added a regression test to `PackageUriHandlingTests` (opens a package with a malformed relationship from a file path in read-only mode and asserts the bytes are unchanged); it fails without the change and passes with it. The Framework, Framework.Features and Packaging test projects pass on net8.0. Happy to open a PR if this approach looks right.

**Desktop**

 - OS: Windows 11
 - .NET Target: .NET 8.0
 - DocumentFormat.OpenXml Version: 3.5.1 (also seen on 3.3.0)

**Additional context**

Related, but different: #1962 (malformed URIs not rewritten) and #2081 (read-only stream open needing a writable temp location). The rewriting itself is expected on .NET 8; the problem is that a read-only open by path persists it.

