### Describe the bug

Since 3.4.0, reading or setting a strongly typed child property (for example `Paragraph.ParagraphProperties`, `Run.RunProperties`, `RunProperties.Bold`) allocates for every child element of the parent, and the setters became one to two orders of magnitude slower than in 3.3.0.

The cause is `OpenXmlSchemaType.Equals(OpenXmlSchemaType)`, introduced with #1842 (`src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Framework/OpenXmlSchemaType.cs`):

```csharp
public bool Equals(OpenXmlSchemaType other) => Equals(Name, other.Name) && Equals(Type, other.Type);
```

Inside the struct, the two-argument `Equals(Name, other.Name)` binds to the static `object.Equals(object, object)`, so both `OpenXmlQualifiedName` values are boxed on every comparison (two allocations of 32 bytes on x64, plus the virtual `Equals(object)` and the unboxing). `operator ==` forwards to this method.

The comparison is on the hot path of the typed child properties:

- `ParticleExtensions.Get` compares `child.Metadata.Type == type` for every child until it finds the requested one (for an absent child: every child).
- `ParticleExtensions.Set` -> `ParticleCollection.Clear()` and `GetNode()` call `CompiledParticle.Find(child)` for every child, and `Find` scans the parent's lookup table (61 entries for `w:p`) with the same comparison.

A single `paragraph.ParagraphProperties = new ParagraphProperties()` on a paragraph with 256 runs allocates about 2.9 MB.

### To Reproduce

Synthetic content: 200 paragraphs with N runs each (`rPr` + `t`), every second paragraph with a `pPr`. Each benchmark method touches 100 paragraphs (`SetRunProperties`: every run).

```csharp
foreach (var p in withPPr) p.ParagraphProperties = new ParagraphProperties();   // SetReplace
foreach (var p in withoutPPr) _ = p.ParagraphProperties;                         // GetAbsent
foreach (var r in runs) r.RunProperties = new RunProperties();                   // SetRunProperties
```

<details>
<summary>BenchmarkDotNet class</summary>

```csharp
[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class TypedChildProperties
{
    private Paragraph[] _withPPr, _withoutPPr;
    private Run[] _runs;

    [Params(4, 32, 256)]
    public int Runs { get; set; }

    [GlobalSetup]
    public void Setup()
    {
        var body = new Body();
        for (var i = 0; i < 200; i++)
        {
            var p = new Paragraph();
            if (i % 2 == 0)
            {
                p.AppendChild(new ParagraphProperties(new ParagraphStyleId { Val = "Normal" }));
            }

            for (var j = 0; j < Runs; j++)
            {
                p.AppendChild(new Run(new RunProperties(new Bold()), new Text("text " + j)));
            }

            body.AppendChild(p);
        }

        var paragraphs = body.Elements<Paragraph>().ToArray();
        _withPPr = paragraphs.Where((_, i) => i % 2 == 0).ToArray();
        _withoutPPr = paragraphs.Where((_, i) => i % 2 == 1).ToArray();
        _runs = body.Descendants<Run>().ToArray();
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public int GetAbsent() => _withoutPPr.Count(p => p.ParagraphProperties != null);

    [Benchmark]
    public void SetReplace()
    {
        foreach (var p in _withPPr)
        {
            p.ParagraphProperties = new ParagraphProperties();
        }
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public void SetRunProperties()
    {
        foreach (var r in _runs)
        {
            r.RunProperties = new RunProperties();
        }
    }
}
```

The SDK version is a build parameter (`<PackageReference Include="DocumentFormat.OpenXml" Version="$(OxVersion)" />`, one BenchmarkDotNet job per version via `WithMsBuildArguments`), so every version runs in its own process.

</details>

### Observed behavior

.NET 10.0.2, x64, BenchmarkDotNet 0.15.8, values per benchmark invocation (100 paragraphs), time / allocated. `main` = 431ab05c built locally, `fix` = `main` plus the change below; the first four columns come from one session.

| Benchmark | 2.10.1 | 3.5.1 | main | fix |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| SetReplace, N=32 | 15 us / 14 KB | 9,040 us / 36.3 MB | 14,008 us / 36.3 MB | 1,925 us / 13 KB |
| SetReplace, N=256 | 227 us / 14 KB | 112,680 us / 290 MB | 109,158 us / 290 MB | 14,858 us / 13 KB |
| SetRunProperties, N=256 (51,200 runs) | 11.2 ms / 7.4 MB | 85.7 ms / 65.5 MB | 93.0 ms / 65.5 MB | 23.2 ms / 6.6 MB |
| GetAbsent, N=256 | 12 us / 0 B | 817 us / 1.6 MB | 803 us / 1.6 MB | 388 us / 0 B |

3.3.0 is not affected (SetReplace N=256: 7.3 ms / 9 KB), 3.4.0 is (107 ms / 290 MB), measured in a separate session.

.NET Framework 4.8 shows the same pattern (SetReplace N=256: 3.3.0 12.4 ms / 9 KB, 3.5.1 190 ms / 291 MB, fix 115 ms / 13 KB; SetRunProperties N=256: 3.3.0 24 ms, 3.5.1 145 ms, fix 78 ms). There the fix removes the allocations but only halves the time; the per-child work that replaced the `System.Type` comparison of 3.3.0 (`Metadata` through the feature collection, copies of the 32-byte `OpenXmlSchemaType`) stays comparatively expensive.

In a document generation engine that copies run and paragraph formatting property by property (`rPr.Bold = ...`, `rPr.Italic = ...`), a Word document with a table of contents allocated 1,227 MB per generation on 3.5.1, 156 MB on 2.10.1 and 164 MB with the fix.

### Expected behavior

Typed child property access should not allocate, as in 3.3.0 and 2.x.

### Proposed fix

```csharp
public bool Equals(OpenXmlSchemaType other) => Name.Equals(other.Name) && Type.Equals(other.Type);
```

`OpenXmlQualifiedName` already implements `IEquatable<OpenXmlQualifiedName>` with ordinal comparison, so the semantics stay the same and only the boxing goes away. I will open a PR with this change, a unit test (equality semantics and a zero-allocation assertion on .NET) and a benchmark.

### Possible follow-up (not part of the fix)

The remaining gap to 2.x for large parents comes from the linear particle lookup introduced in 2.11.0 (#676): every get/set walks all children instead of stopping behind the requested position.

- `CompiledParticle.Find` scans `Lookup` linearly for every child; for large particles (`w:p`: 61 entries) a lazily built dictionary keyed by `OpenXmlSchemaType` would make it O(1).
- `ParticleCollection.Set` walks the children twice (`Clear` and `GetNode`); one pass could remove the old child and find the insertion point.

### Desktop

- DocumentFormat.OpenXml 3.4.0 - 3.5.1 and current `main`
- .NET 10 and .NET Framework 4.8, Windows x64
